al  relativity has speed in it twice  one on the top of the fraction and one  on the bottom  we have to watch both as a human  approaches the speed of light  the bottom of the fraction approaches  zero as a result  the momentum approaches infinity but  that's actually only true  if the top of the fraction is a normal  number in our case  the mass of a human times the speed of  light  massless particles like photons found a  loophole to this problem  the indeterminate form that's when some  combination of zero  and infinity can unexpectedly give you a  finite non-zero number  the standard indeterminate forms are  zero over zero  infinity over infinity zero times  infinity  infinity minus infinity zero to the zero  power  and infinity to the zero power that  first one  is the loophole photons use it might be  okay to have a zero on the bottom of a  fraction  as long as you also have one on top  photons do that by having zero mass  that's what allows them to travel at the  speed of light and not have infinite  momentum  a zero mass gives us zero over zero  an indeterminate form and the result is  a finite  non-zero momentum kinda cool huh  anyway some weird stuff happens as you  approach the speed of light  length contracts time dilates it's crazy  counterintuitive  if you're unfamiliar with it and not  only that those effects  are relative as we watch this  accelerating rocket shrink in length  and see its clock slow down any  passenger on that rocket will see the  same thing  happen to the rest of the universe the  entire universe will contract  along the direction of motion and it  will take more time for things to happen  in that universe  but remember the rocket can never  actually reach the speed of light  so time will never actually stop the  clock  will always be ticking forward just  extremely slowly  but a photon can go at exactly the speed  of light i mean  it can't go any other speed plugging any  other speed  into this equation would make a photon's  momentum zero  which means it wouldn't exist photons  have to travel at the speed of light or  they don't exist  and that has relativistic consequences  if you take this effect to the extreme  limit  the universe will flatten to zero  thickness and time  will dilate to infinity for a freely  traveling photon  the moment it's created is the moment  it's destroyed  and those events happen at the same  place which is the only place  nothing would be happening in the  surrounding universe because the  surrounding universe wouldn't exist  so what would it be like to be made of  photons well if you're a collection of  photons  everything is basically the same the  confinement of those photons would still  give you mass  in exactly the same way your gluons  already do  you would still experience time but if  somehow you are a single photon freely  moving through the universe  that's when things get weird as massive  things approach the speed of light  the rest of the universe shrinks and  slows  as a single photon you would take this  effect to its inevitable limit  and the surrounding universe would cease  to exist  photons don't experience anything  photons  are timeless so would you want to be  made of light  please share in the comments thanks for  liking and sharing this video  a special thanks goes out to patreon  patrons like matthew o'connor  who's been supporting us at the asylum  counselor level for over a year  wow that's a lot of support don't forget  to subscribe if you'd like to keep up  with us  until next time remember it's okay to be  a little crazy  do you want to learn something new well  brilliant is the place for you  it's a website and app that makes  learning accessible and fun  brilliance courses are laid out like a  story and broken into pieces  so you can tackle them one bit at a time  and there are over 60 of them  so there's something for everyone  brilliant calculus fundamentals course  begins with a quiz on indeterminate  forms and is part of one o